  "output": "Now I have enough to produce a thorough, accurate analysis.\n\n---\n\n## Performance Analysis\n\nNo React is present — this is a vanilla JS frontend. The points below cover the actual stack (FastAPI, aiosqlite, Gemini client, vanilla JS).\n\n---\n\n### 1. N+1 Database Connection Pattern (High Impact)\n\n**Location:** `src/data/db.py` — every method\n\nEvery DB operation opens and closes a new `aiosqlite` connection. During a single query, `process_query` (`websocket.py:162,182`) calls `save_query` twice, `save_orchestrator_response` once, and each specialist cache miss triggers `get_cached` → `set_cached` (and `get_stale` on error). With 8 specialists each fetching 4 URLs, a cold run can open **50–100 separate SQLite connections**.\n\n**Fix:** Use a single persistent connection with WAL mode, opened at app startup.\n\n```python\n# src/data/db.py — replace per-operation connect with a shared connection\n\nclass DatabaseManager:\n    def __init__(self, db_path: Path = _DB_PATH) -> None:\n        self._db_path = db_path\n        self._db_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        self._conn: aiosqlite.Connection | None = None\n\n    async def initialize(self) -> None:\n        self._conn = await aiosqlite.connect(self._db_path)\n        await self._conn.execute(\"PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL\")\n        await self._conn.execute(\"PRAGMA synchronous=NORMAL\")\n        await self._conn.executescript(_SCHEMA)\n        await self._conn.commit()\n\n    async def close(self) -> None:\n        if self._conn:\n            await self._conn.close()\n            self._conn = None\n\n    async def save_query(self, record: QueryRecord) -> None:\n        await self._conn.execute(\n            \"INSERT OR REPLACE INTO queries (...) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)\",\n            (...),\n        )\n        await self._conn.commit()\n```\n\nWire `initialize()` and `close()` into `src/main.py` lifespan, and inject the shared `DatabaseManager` instance throughout.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Regex Compiled on Every URL Fetch (Medium Impact)\n\n**Location:** `src/fetch/engine.py:69`\n\n```python\n# CURRENT — compiles a new Pattern object on every fetch() call\ndef _normalize_url(self, url: str) -> str:\n    import re\n    if re.search(r'laws-lois\\.justice\\.gc\\.ca/eng/acts/[^/]+/$', url):\n        ...\n```\n\nThe `import re` inside the method is also repeated work (though Python caches it, the attribute lookup still happens).\n\n**Fix:** Module-level compiled constant.\n\n```python\nimport re\n\n_LAWS_ACT_RE = re.compile(r'laws-lois\\.justice\\.gc\\.ca/eng/acts/[^/]+/$')\n\nclass FetchEngine:\n    def _normalize_url(self, url: str) -> str:\n        if _LAWS_ACT_RE.search(url):\n            return url.rstrip('/') + '/FullText.html'\n        return url\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. GenerativeModel Instantiated on Every LLM Call (Medium Impact)\n\n**Location:** `src/llm/client.py:40–50`, called from `chat()`, `stream_chat()`, `chat_parsed()`\n\n`_make_model()` creates a new `genai.GenerativeModel` object on every request. The model object is stateless relative to the config, so it can be cached.\n\n```python\nclass GeminiClient:\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        ...\n        # Pre-build the two variants used in practice\n        self._model = self._make_model()\n        self._model_json = self._make_model(json_output=True)\n\n    async def chat(self, system_prompt: str, user_message: str, model: str | None = None) -> str:\n        m = self._model if model is None else self._make_model(model)\n        contents = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"parts\": [f\"{system_prompt}\\n\\n{user_message}\"]}]\n        return await self._generate(m, contents)\n\n    async def chat_parsed(self, system_prompt: str, user_message: str, model: str | None = None) -> dict:\n        m = self._model_json if model is None else self._make_model(model, json_output=True)\n        ...\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. `stream_chat` Holds Semaphore for Full Stream Duration (Medium Impact)\n\n**Location:** `src/llm/client.py:84–89`\n\n```python\nasync with self._semaphore:           # held for entire stream\n    response = await m.generate_content_async(contents, stream=True)\n    async for chunk in response:\n        yield text\n```\n\nWith `_MAX_CONCURRENT=3`, a 30-second stream blocks two other concurrent callers for 30 seconds. The semaphore should only gate the initial API call, not the streaming consumption.\n\n```python\nasync def stream_chat(self, system_prompt: str, user_message: str, model: str | None = None) -> AsyncGenerator[str, None]:\n    m = self._model\n    contents = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"parts\": [f\"{system_prompt}\\n\\n{user_message}\"]}]\n    async with self._semaphore:\n        response = await m.generate_content_async(contents, stream=True)\n    # semaphore released; stream the chunks outside\n    async for chunk in response:\n        text = _safe_text(chunk)\n        if text:\n            yield text\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. LLM Response Cache Opportunity (High Impact)\n\nThere is no caching of LLM responses. If two users ask the same (or a very similar) policy question within a session, the full multi-agent pipeline (2 LLM calls for routing + N specialist LLM calls + 1 synthesis call) re-runs.\n\n**Fix:** Add an in-process LRU cache keyed on `(query_text_normalized)` for the orchestrator synthesis, backed by the existing SQLite store:\n\n```python\n# src/agents/orchestrator.py\nimport functools, hashlib\n\n_response_cache: dict[str, OrchestratorResponse] = {}\n_CACHE_MAX = 100\n\nasync def process_with_streaming(self, query_text: str, ...) -> OrchestratorResponse:\n    cache_key = hashlib.sha256(query_text.strip().lower().encode()).hexdigest()\n    if cache_key in _response_cache:\n        logger.info(\"Cache hit for query %s\", cache_key[:8])\n        # Still emit status callbacks so the UI shows something\n        await status_callback(WSAgentStatusUpdate(agent_id=\"orchestrator\", status=\"complete\"))\n        return _response_cache[cache_key]\n\n    result = await self._run_full_pipeline(query_text, fetch_engine, status_callback)\n\n    if len(_response_cache) >= _CACHE_MAX:\n        _response_cache.pop(next(iter(_response_cache)))\n    _response_cache[cache_key] = result\n    return result\n```\n\nFor a production deployment, substitute `functools.lru_cache` or a Redis-backed store.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Redundant `save_query` Call (Low-Medium Impact)\n\n**Location:** `src/api/websocket.py:162` and `websocket.py:182`\n\n`save_query` is called twice per query — once before processing (with empty `agents_invoked`) and once after (with results). Both open a separate DB connection. The first save exists only to record the query exists before the pipeline starts, but there is no consumer that reads the query record mid-flight.\n\n**Fix:** Remove the pre-save if no mid-flight reads are needed. If crash recovery logging is desired, batch both fields into a single `INSERT` at the end, or write a lightweight `_save_query_start` that omits the `agents_invoked` and `processing_time_ms` columns and uses a partial update.\n\n---\n\n### 7. `isProcessing()` DOM Traversal on Every Keypress (Low Impact)\n\n**Location:** `static/js/app.js:63–65` calls `isProcessing()` → `agentList.querySelector(...)` on every `input` event.\n\n```js\n// CURRENT — DOM traversal on every keystroke\nfunction isProcessing() {\n    return !!currentQueryId &&\n           agentList.querySelector('.status-dot[data-status=\"working\"]') !== null;\n}\n```\n\n**Fix:** Maintain a boolean flag updated at state transitions.\n\n```js\nlet _processing = false;\n\nfunction setProcessing(val) {\n    _processing = val;\n    sendButton.disabled = val || queryInput.value.trim().length === 0;\n    sendButton.style.opacity = (!val && queryInput.value.trim()) ? '1' : '0.6';\n}\n\nfunction isProcessing() { return _processing; }\n\n// In submitQuery():\nsetProcessing(true);\n\n// In resetProcessingState():\nsetProcessing(false);\n```\n\n---\n\n### 8. `wrapWithCitations` Regex Risk (Low-Medium Impact)\n\n**Location:** `static/js/app.js:482–493`\n\n```js\nresult = result.replace(\n    new RegExp(`(${search.replace(...)}[\\\\s\\\\S]*?)`, 'gi'),\n    `$1${marker}`\n);\n```\n\n`[\\s\\S]*?` with a lazy quantifier after a partial-title anchor can backtrack catastrophically on long responses (>10,000 chars). Additionally, a new `RegExp` is constructed per citation on every call. This function is also called inside `updateMessageWithMetadata` which is currently unreachable but if ever wired up would run on every chunk.\n\n**Fix:** Use a simpler non-backtracking replacement, or skip the inline citation markup entirely (the citations panel in the sidebar already shows full references):\n\n```js\nfunction wrapWithCitations(text, citations) {\n    // Simple approach: append inline superscripts after exact title matches only\n    let result = text;\n    citations.forEach((citation, index) => {\n        const title = citation.instrument_title;\n        if (!title) return;\n        // Escaped literal match only — no [\\s\\S]*? backtracking\n        const escaped = title.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\\]\\\\]/g, '\\\\$&');\n        const re = new RegExp(escaped, 'gi');\n        result = result.replace(re, `$&<sup class=\"citation-marker\" title=\"${title}\">${index + 1}</sup>`);\n    });\n    return result;\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\n| Issue | File | Impact | Effort |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| New DB connection per operation | `db.py` all methods | High | Medium |\n| No LLM response caching | `orchestrator.py` | High | Low |\n| Regex compiled per URL | `engine.py:69` | Medium | Trivial |\n| GenerativeModel rebuilt per call | `client.py:40` | Medium | Low |\n| Semaphore held during streaming | `client.py:84` | Medium | Low |\n| Duplicate `save_query` call | `websocket.py:162` | Low-Med | Low |\n| DOM traversal on every keypress | `app.js:470` | Low | Low |\n| `wrapWithCitations` regex risk | `app.js:482` | Low-Med | Low |\n\nThe two highest-value fixes are the **DB connection pooling** (items 1) and adding an **LLM response cache** (item 5) — together they would eliminate the dominant latency on repeated or similar queries and reduce SQLite contention under concurrent users.\n",
